--TEST--
Every `Core\Http\Client` member refuses a `tainted` URL and accepts what the launderer answers, so `Core\Http::allowUrl` is the only way one reaches the outbound door — ADR 0058 §§ 1-2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/outbound-url-is-a-sink` makes the URL parameter of every outbound member a sink: the
// declared type is `string | Core\Http\Target`, both halves unqualified, so a
// value that came from outside the process is refused by the type itself and
// not by a check a member could forget.
//
// The depth claim is *agreement*: one question asked of all five members,
// asserting that they answer it the same way. A member that spelled its URL
// `tainted string` — the spelling that reads most natural, since a URL is
// usually assembled from something a caller sent — would still print
// plausibly on its own line and fails here.
tainted string $outside = "http://example.com/status";

var $a = Core\Http\Client::get($outside);
var $b = Core\Http\Client::post($outside);
var $c = Core\Http\Client::put($outside);
var $d = Core\Http\Client::delete($outside);
var $e = Core\Http\Client::head($outside);

// And the door itself, which is the reason the five above can be closed: the
// one parameter in the language that admits a tainted URL is the launderer's,
// and what it answers with is accepted everywhere they refused. These two
// lines contribute no diagnostic, which is half the claim — five refusals with
// no way through them would be a class nobody can call.
Core\Http\Target $pinned = Core\Http::allowUrl($outside);
var $f = Core\Http\Client::get($pinned);
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string|Core\Http\Target`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:14:32
